elmendorf1960 [427]: The immediate purposes to which formal educative procedures were directed were: (1) Disciplinary preparation for attaining a successful guardian-spirit relationship; (2) inculcation of socially valued attitudes and deportment standards. (...) To attain the first educative goal the Twana bathed, scrubbed, and depialted their children's bodies, and on occasion deprived them of food and sleep;

elmendorf1960 [428]: An associated goal was development of habits of rigor in treatment of the body and its demands, a self-control amounting at times to asceticism. The result was, at least in theory, a strenghtening and toughening of both body and character. Older family members waked preadolescent children of both sexes before dawn and supervised, or with younger children enforced, their bath in in cold water, either salt or fresh. The early morning bath was accompanied by vigorous scrubbing of the skin with yew or other conifer boughs. It was carried out at all seasons, even when the half-awake child had to be thrown through the thin river ice which sometimes formed in the middle of winter. Adults continued these dawn immersions throughout life, except when ill or decrepit.

elmendorf1960 [429]: The  commonest of all methods of punishing a recalcitrant or disobedient child was to deprive it of food; HA said that on occasion such punitive fasts might last up to 10 days! Depriving a child of water was also a punitive measure, sometimes applied conjointly with starving. Deprivation was approved as a constructive type of discipline;

elmendorf1960 [433]: Boys. Freedom of male children increased in many ways after the age of approximately eight; at least they were not kept indoors every evening, although the physical side of their training proceeded with undiminished rigor.
